{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "../../dist/out-tsc/libs/platform",
    "declaration": true,
    "declarationMap": true,
    "inlineSources": true,
    "sourceMap": true,
    "types": [],

    // `tsconfig.base.json` does **not** set `strict`; every library sets it in its own
    // `tsconfig.json`. This file extends the base directly, so without restating these
    // the whole package was compiled with `strictNullChecks: false` — which silently
    // collapsed `string | null` to `string` and shipped 8 wrong nullable types in the
    // public API. A customer calling `ruleContext.username.set(null)` on sign-out got a
    // compile error that our own source tree does not produce.
    //
    // Nothing caught it: `test` does not typecheck, `build` and `typecheck` run against
    // the per-library configs which are strict, and the API snapshot recorded the wrong
    // types as the baseline. Only `fork-simulation` — compiling the template against the
    // built declarations — surfaced it. Keep these in step with
    // `libs/shared/*/tsconfig.json`.
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "rootDir": ".."
  },
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    // Partial ("linked") compilation — Angular's library distribution format. It emits
    // `ɵɵngDeclareComponent` calls that the consuming application's linker lowers to the
    // `ɵɵdefineComponent` of *its own* Angular version, which is what lets one published
    // tarball work across minor versions.
    //
    // This is the SAME root cause as the `strict` block above, and that is the lesson:
    // `@nx/angular:package` with a `tsConfig` option calls
    // `parseRemappedTsConfigAndMergeDefaults`, which despite its name merges only eight
    // hardcoded options and otherwise REPLACES ng-packagr's bundled
    // `ng-packagr/src/lib/ts/conf/tsconfig.ngc.json`. That bundled file is the only place
    // `compilationMode: partial` is set; Angular's own default is `FULL`. So this file is
    // not a layer on top of sensible defaults — it is the entire configuration, and
    // anything it fails to restate is lost.
    //
    // The consequence was not subtle and still went unnoticed for a whole phase:
    // ng-packagr writes a `prepublishOnly` script that hard-fails `npm publish` for any
    // full-mode package, so `@nuxeo/satori-platform` could not be published at all —
    // while Phase 4's central claim was that a customer upgrade is an `npm version` bump.
    // `scripts/beta-harness/publishability.mjs` is the gate that now runs a real
    // `npm publish --dry-run` against the built bytes, which is the only check that
    // executes the `prepublishOnly` script this omission caused ng-packagr to write.
    "compilationMode": "partial",
    "enableI18nLegacyMessageIdFormat": false,
    "strictInjectionParameters": true,
    "strictInputAccessModifiers": true,
    "strictTemplates": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "../shared/*/src/**/*.ts"],
  // The `../shared/*` patterns are not redundant. TypeScript resolves `exclude` globs
  // relative to this directory and `**` never matches `..`, so the bare `**/*.spec.ts`
  // silently failed to exclude the specs pulled in by the `../shared/*` include — the
  // 1,457 "Cannot find name 'describe'" errors that appear on a direct `tsc -p` run,
  // every one of them in `libs/shared`. ng-packagr compiles from the public API and so
  // never reached them, which is why the `build` gate stayed green over it.
  "exclude": [
    "**/*.spec.ts",
    "**/*.test.ts",
    "**/test-setup.ts",
    "../shared/*/src/**/*.spec.ts",
    "../shared/*/src/**/*.test.ts",
    "../shared/*/src/**/test-setup.ts"
  ]
}
